      ],
      "text": "**How a Rainbow Forms \u2013 A Detailed Explanation**\n\nA rainbow is a spectacular, circular arc of colored light that appears in the sky after rain. It results from the combined effects of **refraction**, **internal reflection**, and **dispersion** of sunlight within water droplets. To understand the formation, we must follow the path of each photon as it enters, bounces, and exits a spherical raindrop, and then examine how the resulting angles and colors are observed by the viewer.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Sunlight and the Role of the Sun\n\nSunlight is a mixture of many wavelengths (colors) of visible light. When the sun is low on the horizon, its rays strike the droplets of water in the atmosphere. The angle at which the sun is above the horizon determines the position of the rainbow on the ground.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Refraction (Bending) at the First Surface\n\nWhen a light ray enters a water droplet, it slows down and bends toward the normal (the line perpendicular to the surface). This bending is called **refraction**. Because the refractive index of water is higher than that of air, the ray changes direction. The amount of bending depends on the wavelength of the light: shorter wavelengths (blue, violet)",
